<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Knowledge Blog</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">White Truffle, Black Truffle and Summer Truffle: What Is the Difference?</span></nav><article class="lux-reader-layout lux-academy-reader">
    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Truffle Academy</span><span>Truffle Academy · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">White Truffle, Black Truffle and Summer Truffle: What Is the Difference?</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">One of the most common misunderstandings among people discovering truffles for the first time is simple: Aren’t all truffles basically the same, apart from the colour?</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="1334" height="2000" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/truffle-truffle-types.webp'); ?>" alt="White Truffle, Black Truffle and Summer Truffle: What Is the Difference?"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section lux-reader-section-opening"><p>They are not.</p><p>Precious white truffle, precious black truffle, summer truffle, and other edible truffle species are botanically distinct.</p><p>They can differ in seasonality, aroma, culinary behaviour, availability, and market value.</p><p>Italian regulations themselves distinguish between different edible truffle species rather than treating “truffle” as one single category.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Precious White Truffle: When Aroma Becomes the Main Ingredient</h2><p>Precious white truffle is one of the most highly valued truffles associated with Italian gastronomy.</p><p>Its mature aroma can be exceptionally distinctive and complex.</p><p>For this reason, it is commonly used in preparations where the truffle does not need to undergo prolonged cooking.</p><p>Warm dishes such as eggs, pasta, risotto, potatoes, and mild cheeses can provide a neutral background while helping release the aroma of freshly sliced truffle.</p><p>The purpose of these ingredients is not to compete with the white truffle.</p><p>It is to give it enough space to remain clearly recognisable.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>Precious Black Truffle: Deeper and More Integrated Into the Dish</h2><p>Precious black truffle represents another important Italian truffle tradition.</p><p>Its aromatic character is different from that of white truffle and is often perceived as deeper, warmer, and more earthy.</p><p>It can be incorporated into a wider range of preparations and is often associated with meat, potatoes, eggs, cheese, sauces, and pasta.</p><p>The objective is still not to use as much truffle as possible.</p><p>It is to allow its aroma to become an intentional part of the overall dish.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>Summer Truffle: Milder and Highly Versatile</h2><p>Summer truffle generally presents a more delicate aromatic profile than precious white or precious black truffle.</p><p>This does not mean that it has “no flavour.”</p><p>It simply means that it belongs to a different culinary context.</p><p>Summer truffle can work particularly well in pasta, egg dishes, cream-based preparations, meat dishes, sauces, and processed truffle products.</p><p>When a dish needs a recognisable truffle character without an extremely dominant aromatic intensity, summer truffle can offer a useful alternative.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Truffles Have Seasons</h2><p>Different truffle species do not naturally reach maturity at the same time throughout the year.</p><p>Precious white truffles are generally associated with the autumn and winter period.</p><p>Precious black truffles are traditionally associated with winter.</p><p>Summer truffles, as the name suggests, are primarily linked to the warmer months.</p><p>Exact authorised harvesting periods can vary between Italian regions, so there is no single permanent calendar that applies identically throughout the country.</p><p>This is why a proper discussion of seasonal truffles should always ask three questions:</p><p>Which species? Which region? Which harvesting period?</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>Price Is Not the Only Way to Compare Truffles</h2><p>Precious white truffles can reach extremely high market prices.</p><p>That does not mean that every product or every recipe should use white truffle.</p><p>Ingredient choice depends on purpose.</p><p>Someone looking for the experience of freshly shaved truffle may require one type of product.</p><p>Someone preparing a sauce, seasoning, spread, or more stable everyday application may need something completely different.</p><p>The most expensive ingredient is not automatically the most appropriate ingredient for every product.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-5"><h2>LuxurEat(露意膳) Perspective</h2><p>When LuxurEat(露意膳) presents truffle products, the most useful approach is to explain clearly which type of truffle is actually being used rather than relying only on the generic word “truffle.”</p><p>A product page becomes much more meaningful when it explains:</p><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the truffle species;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the percentage used when relevant;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the main supporting ingredients;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>the expected flavour profile;</li></ul><ul class="lux-reader-prose-list"><li>recommended culinary uses.</li></ul><p>This allows the value of LuxurEat(露意膳) products to come from information consumers can understand and verify, rather than relying only on abstract words such as “precious,” “exclusive,” or “luxury.”</p></section></div></section>
